## Measured

Measured on the branch that shipped the closure backlog-delta fields (#993), base `c62e8a3f`.

- **`parseGapSection` has two return shapes, and one of them is overloaded.**
  `scripts/kaola-workflow-gap-sweep.js:234-294` returns `null` when the summary file is absent or the
  `## Run gaps` heading never appears, and returns an array otherwise. It returns `[]` **both** when
  the section is genuinely empty and when every bullet in it fails the strict row regex
  (`/^-\s+(\S+)\s+\((.+?)\):\s+(filed:\s*#(\d+)|noise:\s+(.+))$/`, `:265`).
- **The shipped closure stamp therefore has two states, not three.** `null` → `follow_ups_filed`,
  `follow_up_numbers`, `net_backlog_delta` all `unknown`; any array → measured, `filed:` rows counted.
  So a section whose rows are all malformed stamps `follow_ups_filed: 0` — a measured-looking zero for
  a section nothing could read.
- **Free-text bullets are ignored deliberately and must NOT be treated as evidence of malformation.**
  `gap-sweep.js:267-281` states free-text bullets are ignored by design for back-compat and "must
  never warn". `test-finalize-door.js` T14's `freetext` leg pins this: a section containing `- none`
  asserts `follow_ups_filed: '0'` and `follow_up_numbers: 'none'`. A bullet-counting detector is
  therefore wrong, and was tried and rejected during #993 for exactly this reason.
- **Malformation is not fully silent today.** `gap-sweep.js:275-280` writes a stderr advisory for a
  line that matches the loose `/^-\s+.*\(.*\):\s*(filed:|noise:)/` shape but fails the strict regex.
  That advisory is emitted at sweep time and is not carried into the closure receipt.
- **Rows failing the strict grammar are NOT theoretical — there is a closed precedent.** #726,
  *"parseGapSection grammar rejects any `## Run gaps` sample containing `)` — a swept manual gap with
  a paren is silently unmappable (gaps_unswept)"*. That is a documented real case of rows the parser
  could not map. What has **not** been observed is the new closure stamp mis-reporting because of it —
  the stamp shipped with #993 and has no history yet.

searched: `gh search issues --repo KaolaBrother/Kaola-Workflow "parseGapSection"` → **1 hit, #726
(closed)**, the paren-in-sample grammar bug whose fix is the lazy quantifier now guarded by
"Do not 'simplify' this quantifier" — same parser, adjacent failure, not this conflation.
`"malformed"` → 4+ hits, nearest **#848 (closed)** *"Four conditions say 'anchor absent' when the
value was present and failed a shape check"* — **the same conflation class generalized** (absent vs
present-but-unparseable), so this issue is a fresh instance of a pattern this repo has already named
and fixed elsewhere, and #848 is the design precedent to read first. `"Run gaps"` → #992/#993 (this
bundle) plus #971/#681/#868 on unrelated gap-sweep surfaces. Not a duplicate of any.

## Hypothesis

- That a fully-malformed `## Run gaps` section is rare in practice rather than impossible. #726 shows
  individual unmappable rows happen; whether a section ever had **all** rows unmappable is untested.
  Claim to test: scan archived runs' `finalization-summary.md` for sections where the loose pattern
  matched but the strict one did not.
- That the honest fix belongs **inside** `parseGapSection` rather than beside it, because only the
  parser knows which lines it attempted to map. Not established — an alternative is a separate
  reported count rather than a changed return shape.

## Proposed remedy (non-binding)

Give `parseGapSection` a way to report that it saw rows it attempted to map and could not, so the
closure stamp can degrade those to `unknown` alongside the section-absent case. Whatever the shape, it
must not disturb the strict regex — the comment at `:256-264` ends "Do not 'simplify' this quantifier"
— nor the free-text-bullet behaviour pinned by T14, nor `samplesMatch`, nor either refusal direction
(`gaps_unswept`, `observed_gap_unseeded`). Deliberately deferred once already: during #993 the owner
ruled that two states ship and this lands as its own slice, rather than reshaping a parser the scanner
owns late in an unrelated change.
